content.thinking:
Moved. Now create the Block Grid data type. Editor alias `Umbraco.BlockGrid`, editorUiAlias `Umb.PropertyEditorUi.BlockGrid`. Values:
- gridColumns: "12"
- blocks: array of { contentElementTypeKey: <key> } for the three element types.

The blocks config item shape in Umbraco v13+ BlockGridConfiguration: BlockConfiguration has `contentElementTypeKey`, `settingsElementTypeKey`, `label`, `thumbnail`, `view`, `stylesheet`, `overlaySize`. I'll provide contentElementTypeKey only (plus maybe label). Save at root — DataTypeSavedHandler files it under /Umbraco/Umbraco.BlockGrid (built-in editor).

Then create the two templates. Template content: I'll put the final Razor content in the DB templates too (same as files I'll write later). Actually — let me reconsider. Writing full final content into DB templates now means I duplicate the view content. If the file wins at render time (likely in v13+: file-based templates take precedence? or DB?), identical content = identical result either way. But the DB content must be valid Razor that compiles — including `@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlogFrontPage>` requires the model to exist at runtime compile. DB templates compile at runtime when first requested — after restart+build, models exist. Before that, pages would error — but we won't request pages before restart. OK.

Hmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider whether DB template content even gets used when a file exists. In Umbraco 13+:
- Templates can be "file-based" (a .cshtml in Views/) or stored in DB.
- When rendering, Umbraco resolves the view for the template alias. The view engine looks in ~/Views/{alias}.cshtml. 
- For DB templates, Umbraco has a mechanism: `DatabaseTemplateViewEngine`? Hmm... Actually I recall in v13 the template content is rendered through the same Razor view engine by writing... no.

Let me think about what actually happens in v13+: The `TemplateController`/`PublishedContentRequestBuilder` maps the template to a view name (the alias). The Razor view engine then looks for a view named e.g. "BlogFrontPage". Views come from: (a) .cshtml files under ~/Views (compiled into the app or runtime-compiled), (b) DB templates via `Umbraco.Cms.Web.Common.ViewEngines` — there IS a composite view engine that includes a "template from database" provider? 

I genuinely recall: In Umbraco 13, templates created in the backoffice (DB) render fine without files — the content is compiled via Razor runtime compilation from the DB string. And if a file with the same alias exists, the FILE takes precedence (the docs for v13 migration say file-based templates continue to work and take precedence over DB ones? or they conflict?).

Hmm. To eliminate risk: I'll set the DB template content to the same final markup as the file. Then whichever wins, output is identical. The only wrinkle: content with `@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlogFrontPage>` + `@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models` — fine in both.

But wait — there's a subtlety with runtime compilation of DB templates: they compile against the app assembly, so `Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models.BlogFrontPage` resolves after build. Good.

Alternatively, even simpler: make the DB template content a tiny placeholder that redirects... no, keep identical content.

Actually, hmm, one more consideration: when a template file exists AND a DB template exists with the same alias, Umbraco v13+ backoffice shows the template as "file-based" and ignores DB content? I believe file wins. Either way identical content = safe.

Let me now write the actual view content. I'll design the views now (they'll be written to disk in step 3, and embedded in DB templates now).

**Design decisions:**

Layout: no _Layout exists in scaffold. I'll create `Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml`? Hmm — "Views/Shared/Components/ holds only ViewComponent view folders — never loose partials." A _Layout.cshtml in Views/Shared/ root is a loose file... The rule says Views/Shared/Components/ holds only component view folders. Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml is a layout, not a partial — but to be safe, avoid _Layout entirely: make each page view self-contained (full HTML document). Two page templates, each self-contained. Simpler, fewer conventions to trip. But duplication of <head>... acceptable for a demo. Actually, I could use a partial for the head... "never loose partials" applies to Views/Shared/Components/ only. A _Head.cshtml partial in Views/Shared/ would be fine? The rule literally constrains Views/Shared/Components/. Hmm, but to keep it dead simple and convention-safe: self-contained pages. The duplication is small (link + script tags + nav header). Actually let me do a small shared partial `Views/Shared/_SiteHeader.cshtml`? Hmm, "No loose files in Models/" is the Models rule; for Views there's no such rule except Components/. I'll keep pages fully self-contained — zero risk.

Wait, actually — should I even use Tailwind via dist/site.css? The frontend build completed (npm install + build exit 0), so wwwroot/dist/site.css + main.js should exist. The Tailwind build scanned Views/**/*.cshtml — at build time, my new views didn't exist yet! Tailwind (v4 with @source scanning) generates utilities found in scanned files. My page/component views use Tailwind classes that DON'T exist in any scanned file yet → those utilities won't be in site.css!

So after writing the views in step 3, I must REBUILD the frontend (npm run build) so Tailwind picks up the new classes. Or run `npm run watch` in background. I'll rebuild after writing views. Good catch.

**View content plan:**

BlogFrontPage.cshtml (self-contained):
```cshtml
@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models
@using Umbraco.Extensions
@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlogFrontPage>
@{
    Layout = null;
    var posts = Model.Children<BlogPost>().OrderByDescending(p => p.PublishDate).ToList();
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>@Model.Name</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/dist/site.css" asp-append-version="true" />
    <script type="module" src="~/dist/main.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
</head>
<body class="bg-slate-50 text-slate-900">
    @await Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "blocks")
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Latest posts</h2>
        @if (posts.Count == 0)
        {
            <p class="text-slate-500">No posts yet.</p>
        }
        else
        {
            <ul class="space-y-6">
                @foreach (var post in posts)
                {
                    <li>
                        <a href="@post.Url()" class="block rounded-lg bg-white p-6 shadow hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-indigo-700">@post.Name</h3>
                            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600">@post.Summary</p>
                            <time class="mt-2 block text-sm text-slate-400" datetime="@post.PublishDate.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")">@post.PublishDate.ToString("MMMM d, yyyy")</time>
                        </a>
                    </li>
                }
            </ul>
        }
    </main>
</body>
</html>
```

BlogPost.cshtml:
```cshtml
@using Umbraco.Bench.Umbraco.Models
@using Umbraco.Extensions
@inherits UmbracoViewPage<BlogPost>
@{
    Layout = null;
    var frontPage = Model.AncestorOrSelf<BlogFrontPage>();
}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>@Model.Name | Blog</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/dist/site.css" asp-append-version="true" />
    <script type="module" src="~/dist/main.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
</head>
<body class="bg-slate-50 text-slate-900">
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
        <a href="@(frontPage?.Url() ?? "/")" class="text-indigo-700 hover:underline">&larr; All posts</a>
        <header class="mt-6">
            <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">@Model.Name</h1>
            <time class="mt-2 block text-sm text-slate-400" datetime="@Model.PublishDate.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")">@Model.PublishDate.ToString("MMMM d, yyyy")</time>
        </header>
        @await Html.GetBlockGridHtmlAsync(Model, "blocks")
    </main>
</body>
</html>
```

Wait — `Model.AncestorOrSelf<BlogFrontPage>()` — friendly extension AncestorOrSelf<T>(). The post's ancestor is the front page. Good. If null, fallback "/".

Hmm — PublishDate: Date Picker (Umbraco.DateTime, date only, no time). Value "2026-10-08". Generated model property: `DateTime PublishDate`. OrderByDescending works. If value format wrong, could be DateTime.MinValue — I'll verify after creation via get-document-by-id.

